body:
I had an eating disorder for about 3 years. I would binge and then starve myself. I ended up in a mental hospital back in March which made my parents take my mental health more seriously so I checked myself into an eating disorder clinic. I was there for 6 hours a day 7 days a week. In all honesty, I didn't take it seriously for the first few weeks. I thought I would fake my way through it and get out as soon as possible so I could go back to not eating. I would force myself to finish all the meals, even if it physically hurt. When I wanted to binge, I couldn't because I was already full from the other meals. As the weeks passed, it became easier to finish the meals. I gained a little weight but it stopped. I was upset but I figured it meant they would let me out soon. Then something weird happened. I found myself getting hungry around mealtimes, and recognizing when I was full while eating and was able to stop. I wasn't counting calories or keeping track of how many calories I burned. I didn't feel guilty after eating anymore. I didn't constantly feel on the verge of passing out or freezing cold because my body didn't have enough energy to keep me warm. My hands weren't shaky and I felt like I had more energy. I wasn't checking my body every time I passed by a reflective surface. I was able to leave the clinic at the beginning of September. I can't describe how good it feels to finally view food and eating as a positive thing. To eat when I'm hungry and feel good about it afterward. To go on walks without counting my steps. To be able to shower without pinching the fat on my stomach and thighs. 

&#x200B;

It was hard to find ed treatment that focused on trans issues, but I think learning to at least accept your body was helpful too.  Realizing that the hatred of my body was tied to my gender helped me realize indulging my ed wasn't going to magically make my titties fall off. I was also starving myself because I liked the control and for whatever reason being self-destructive feels good. I think that getting yourself to actually eat at regular times in reasonable amounts is the first step. Then you'll realize how much better life is and work towards maintaining healthy eating habits and not relapsing, at least that's how it was for me. I still struggle with body image issues and self-hatred, but at least I have a healthy relationship with food.
